<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Yesterday Was Wednesday.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support. Interactive: Respond in a predictable pattern to simple questions about familiar things.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow with visual support the main plot of simple stories about the days of the week (e.g., "On Monday, I get ready for school."). Can listen and respond in a predictable pattern to simple questions about days of the week, including future references (e.g., "Today is Monday. What day is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Tuesday."). Can follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support by identifying familiar routines associated with specific days (e.g., "On Fridays, we go to the library.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge, introduces new vocabulary and sounds, and checks initial understanding.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a large, colorful calendar. The teacher points to "today" and asks, "What day is today?" Students might respond with a single word or gesture. The teacher then points to the previous day and asks, "What day was yesterday?" (Grammar: Present simple "is", Past simple "was"). The teacher plays a short "Days of the Week" song, encouraging students to listen and clap for each day. Flashcards for Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday are displayed. (Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, today, yesterday).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of the days of the week, especially focusing on /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday." The teacher says each day clearly, pointing to the flashcard and encouraging choral repetition. For example, the teacher says "Wednesday" /wɛnzdeɪ/ and students repeat. The teacher might use exaggerated mouth movements. (Pronunciation: /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday". Phonemic awareness: letter 'W' for /w/ and letter 'F' for /f/).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple Concept Checking Questions.
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows the 'Wednesday' flashcard and asks: "Is this 'Wednesday' or 'Friday'?" (Students point or say the correct word).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the calendar and asks: "Are these days for school?" (Teacher gestures 'yes' or 'no'. Students respond with head nod/shake or "Yes!").</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario. Focuses on providing comprehensible input for listening comprehension.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple audio story (or teacher-read story) titled "A Week at School" presented with a sequence of large, colorful picture cards depicting school activities (e.g., math class, science class, English class, drawing, going to the library, playing with friends). (Genre: narrative excerpt; Register: informal; Format: audio with visual support).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets the main ideas and familiar details in short, simple oral texts related to the days of the week and school routines with visual support.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the story: "Listen to our story about a week at school. Look at the pictures!" (Vocabulary: story, school, week).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays/reads the short story sentence by sentence, displaying the corresponding picture card for each day and activity. For example: "On Monday, we start school. We learn math." (Teacher shows 'Monday' and 'math' picture). "Yesterday was Wednesday. Today is Thursday. On Thursday, we learn science." (Grammar: "On [day]", "Yesterday was...", "Today is..."). "On Friday, I like to draw!" (Pragmatic: Expressing preferences).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The story includes phrases like: "On Monday, we learn math.", "On Tuesday, we have English.", "Yesterday was Wednesday and it was sunny. We went outside!", "Today is Thursday.", "On Friday, we go to the library." (Vocabulary: math, science, English, draw, like, have, learn, library, sunny, friend, yesterday, today, on).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (Listening & Pointing):</b> After the story, the teacher asks, "What is the story about? Days of the week or animals?" (Students point to days of the week flashcard).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Listening & Pointing):</b> The teacher says a day from the story (e.g., "On Friday...") and students point to the picture that shows what happens on Friday (e.g., 'library' picture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Listening & Gesturing):</b> The teacher makes a statement about the story (e.g., "On Monday, we have math." – True, students nod; "On Thursday, we go to the library." – False, students shake heads).</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Students practice recognizing and understanding target language in controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of days of the week, school activities, past simple with "be" (was), present simple with "be" (is), and simple WH-questions (What day is it?).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Listen and Point:</b> The teacher places flashcards of the days of the week and school activities (math, English, draw, library) on the board or floor. The teacher says a sentence, and students listen and point to the correct flashcard. E.g., Teacher says: "Listen: On Tuesday, we have English class." Students point to 'Tuesday' and 'English' cards. Teacher says: "Yesterday was Wednesday." Students point to 'Wednesday' card. (Vocabulary: all days, school subjects/activities; Grammar: "On [day]", "Yesterday was...").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Listen and Match/Circle:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different days/activities and the corresponding day names (Monday, Tuesday, etc.) or simple icons (e.g., a book for "learn," a pencil for "draw"). The teacher reads short sentences or questions. Students listen and match the sentence to the picture by drawing a line, or circle the correct day/activity. E.g., Teacher says: "Today is Thursday. What day is tomorrow?" Then, "Tomorrow is Friday!" Students circle 'Friday' or draw a line to the 'Friday' picture. (Vocabulary: today, tomorrow; Grammar: "What day is tomorrow?", future reference).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check - Listen and Show:</b> Students work in pairs with a set of small picture cards for the days of the week and a few activity cards (e.g., school, play). Student A says: "Today is Monday." Student B listens and holds up the 'Monday' card. Then Student A asks: "What day was yesterday?" Student B listens and holds up 'Sunday' card. Student A confirms. They take turns. The teacher circulates, listening to student interactions and providing support. (Pragmatic: Answering questions about days; Sociolinguistic: Talking about schedules).</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic "School Days" scenario, focusing on fluency and completing a task.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen to the Class Schedule" - a guided listening and arrangement activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student (or small group) a set of shuffled picture cards for "school," "math," "English," "play with friend," and "library," along with the days of the week cards.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher states: "Today, we will listen to our class schedule for the week!" (Sociolinguistic: Talking about schedules).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher dictates a simple week's schedule, one day at a time, incorporating "yesterday was" and "today is" for context. For example: "On Monday, we start with math. Listen and put 'Monday' and 'math'." "Yesterday was Monday. Today is Tuesday. On Tuesday, we have English." "I like Friday! On Friday, we go to the library." (Vocabulary: schedule, start, end, math, English, library, friend; Grammar: "Yesterday was...", "Today is...", "On [day]...").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen carefully and arrange their cards in the correct sequence on their desks. The teacher repeats each sentence twice slowly.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After dictating the full week, the teacher walks around to observe students' arrangements and provides immediate, simple feedback (e.g., "Good!", "This is Friday?").</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection to gauge learning and identify areas for improvement.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and student pointing task.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Identify." The teacher gives each student a worksheet with pictures of the days of the week (Monday-Friday) and different school activities. The teacher says 3-4 sentences. Students listen and point to the correct picture.
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: Teacher says: "Yesterday was Wednesday." Students point to the 'Wednesday' picture.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: Teacher says: "On Friday, I like to draw." Students point to the 'Friday' picture and the 'drawing' picture.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: Teacher says: "Today is Monday. We learn math." Students point to 'Monday' and 'math'.</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of days of the week (pointing).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct association of activities with specific days.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow simple instructions involving "yesterday" and "today."</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a paper with two simple questions with drawing options.
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I hear the days of the week?" (Draw a happy face or a sad face).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is my favorite day to hear?" (Students draw a simple picture of their favorite day or activity, e.g., 'Friday' or 'playing').</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reviews the lesson's effectiveness, guides teacher adaptation, and sets the stage for future learning.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about school days did I hear today?" (Students can point to flashcards or say a word).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to hear the story?" (Students point to a 'happy' or 'struggling' face picture).</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well? (e.g., "The use of TPR and large flashcards effectively engaged the 2nd graders and supported their listening comprehension of new vocabulary and simple sentences. The explicit modeling of /w/ and /f/ was well-received.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Students still confused 'Tuesday' and 'Thursday' pronunciation; I need to provide more auditory discrimination practice. Some students struggled with 'yesterday' vs. 'tomorrow' concept; I should incorporate more visual timelines or gestures next time.").</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "In Lesson 2, we will use the vocabulary we heard today about the days of the week and school activities to practice our *reading comprehension* skills, looking at short texts with pictures about a school week."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>